est* option is to get my pieces extended.\n\n**My final decision is K11.**\n\nBy playing **K11**, I force White to block and this guarantees I get to have an advantage. After that, I can play whatever and white will have a lot less control. I keep the pressure on. D4 can follow.\n\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board shows a "Cross-Sword" style configuration where both Black and White have a diagonal line of 3 stones intersecting or abutting. Black has stones at H8, I9, J10, and White has stones at E5, F6, G7.\nWhite\'s line (E5-G7) is blocked on the bottom-right by Black\'s H8. White threatens to extend to D4 to form a "Sleeping Four" (a line of 4 stones blocked on one side).\nBlack\'s line (H8-J10) is blocked on the top-left by White\'s G7. Black has the opportunity to extend to K11.\n\nReason: The best move for Black is to play K11. This extends Black\'s diagonal line to 4 stones (H8, I9, J10, K11), creating an immediate threat to win at L12 (making 5 in a row). This is a forcing move (Sente) that compels White to defend immediately at L12.\nBy playing K11, Black takes the initiative. If Black were to play defensively at D4 to block White, it would be a passive move. By playing K11 first, Black forces White\'s response and can still play D4 or another strategic move in the subsequent turn, effectively gaining a stone on the board for free while dictating the game\'s flow.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "K", "row": 11}'}
